WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.000
Hi I’m Stephen Wolfram and I’d like to show you a bit about our Wolfram language.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.000
You know, I’ve been working towards what’s now the Wolfram language for about 30  years

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:12.000
and big parts of it I’ve been able to incubate in Mathematica and Wolfram Alpha,

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:17.000
but it’s only in recent times that we’ve finally had what we need to create the whole Wolfram language

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:20.000
and to set up mechanisms to deploy it very widely.

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:25.000
We’re actually at an incredibly exciting point right now where we’re suddenly able to take computation

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:29.000
to a whole new level and in effect, inject sophisticated computation into everything.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
So what is the Wolfram language? Well the first thing to say is that it’s a new kind of thing.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:38.000
It’s what I call a knowledge based language.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:45.000
It’s a language where a vast amount of knowledge about how to do computations and about the world is built right into the language.

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.000
So right within the language there are primitives for processing images

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:55.000
or laying out networks or looking up stock prices or creating interfaces or solving optimization problems or whatever.

00:00:55.000 --> 00:01:01.000
And through the symbolic structure of the language, they’re all set up to work together in a wonderfully coherent way.

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:03.000
Let me show you.

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.000
One of the great things about the Wolfram language is if you want to, you can use it completely interactively.

00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.000
Even on the web.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:15.000
So you can type something in and immediately get a result out, like this, or this.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.000
And yes, it is fast.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.000
Of course it knows how to factor that number too.

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:22.000
It’s got all the fancy algorithms built right in.

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:24.000
it’s also got immediate visualization.

00:01:25.000 --> 00:01:30.000
It also knows right off the bat like, how to get your friend network from Facebook.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.000
It lays out the network automatically and it knows all the fancy graph theory algorithms

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:37.000
so you can immediately ask it all kinds of things about the network,

00:01:37.000 --> 00:01:42.000
like how do your friends break into groups or what’s the most connections any friend has?

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:46.000
The Wolfram language knows how to do lots of things.

00:01:46.000 --> 00:01:51.000
Like lets’ ask it to get the current image from my computers camera. Ah that’s me.

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:56.000
Ok let’s edge detect me. I can have it be dynamic too.

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:06.000
I can do all kinds of computations like there’s me broken into little squares, and there’s me with a random color blended into each square,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.000
and now reassembled.

00:02:08.000 --> 00:02:12.000
The Wolfram language doesn’t just know about algorithms and computations,

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:13.000
it also knows about the world.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.000
Like let’s ask it when the sun will set today.

00:02:17.000 --> 00:02:20.000
There’s the answer for my current location.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:26.000
There’s the time from sunrise to sunset in days. We can convert it to minutes.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:32.000
You know, the Wolfram language has a precise way of talking about everything, but when we are dealing with the world at large,

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:36.000
we often just want to use natural language to describe what we’re talking about.

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:40.000
And thanks to Wolfram alpha, we built a big stack of technology for doing that.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:46.000
Like we can just ask for countries in South America and our natural language understand system can figure out

00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:51.000
what that means and generate precise Wolfram language code from it

00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:55.000
so that we get a list of the countries and now we can compute from that.

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:01.000
Like let’s generate their flags, or let’s find the dominant colors in those flags.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:06.000
Or let’s have a whole little program to make a table of countries and their dominant flag colors.

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Ok let’s try something more ambitious.

00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:13.000
Let’s get a list of the capitals of the countries in Western Europe.

00:03:14.000 --> 00:03:18.000
Now let’s find what order we’d have to visit these in to get the shortest tour of all of them.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:26.000
Needless to say the Wolfram language has the best known algorithms for solving so called traveling salesmen problem like that.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:34.000
Now let’s plot the tour on a map as a thick red line and yes the Wolfram language has detailed maps of everywhere built in.

00:03:35.000 --> 00:03:41.000
Ok well I’ve been doing different kinds of examples, there’s really a huge amount built into the Wolfram language.

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.000
You can get a sense of it here, thousands of algorithmic functions, thousands of domains of data and knowledge,

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:53.000
but in a sense that’s only the beginning because there’s so much more power for being able to put all these things together

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:58.000
and what makes that possible is the structure of the Wolfram language and the principles that it’s built on.

00:03:59.000 --> 00:04:03.000
The first really critical thing to understand about the Wolfram language is that it’s symbolic.

00:04:04.000 --> 00:04:09.000
If you have x, you don’t’ have to give it a value, you can just have it be symbolic x.

00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:16.000
you can type in a formula involving x and then you can do a computation on the formula symbolically.

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:24.000
of course this is great for math but one of the big discoveries of the Wolfram language is that it gives a way to do a zillion other things too.

00:04:24.000 --> 00:04:29.000
Let’s say you’ve got some data with a part you don’t know. You can just treat it symbolically.

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.000
What about something like a network?

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:37.000
Well on the Wolfram language, it’s a symbolic expression too that displays as a graph

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:40.000
but you can go ahead and manipulate it symbolically too,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:45.000
same with 3D graphics. Go ahead and create a symbolic form,

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:54.000
render it as 3D graphics, now because everything is symbolic, I can just pick up this output and feed it into an image processing algorithm

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:57.000
or I can make a table or rotated versions of the image.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:04.000
Something else that’s a symbolic expression in the Wolfram language is the user interface element, like a slider.

00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:09.000
I can make a whole grid of them, build up any user interface, all just represented by a symbolic expression.

00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:13.000
Same with documents and document elements too, all just symbolic.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:17.000
And once things are symbolic, it’s easy to do meta operations on them,

00:05:17.000 --> 00:05:22.000
like take a plot and effectively just pick it up and make a dynamic version of it.

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:30.000
You can make a dynamic version of anything, and because everything is a symbolic expression, it all just works.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:34.000
You know, any function can be treated symbolically.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:43.000
Here’s just f, symbolic f, and actually underneath the full form of everything in the language is like this,

00:05:43.000 --> 00:05:50.000
just a bunch of symbolic functions representing formulas or data sets or documents or cities or whatever.

00:05:50.000 --> 00:05:53.000
So what can you do with these symbolic functions?

00:05:53.000 --> 00:05:56.000
Well one thing is incredibly powerful functional programming.

00:05:56.000 --> 00:06:01.000
Nest a function symbolically or put more and more frames around something.

00:06:01.000 --> 00:06:04.000
Make a kind of nested interface.

00:06:06.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Ok here’s something more ambitious, let’s use functional programming to do some web crawling.

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:16.000
The Wolfram language already knows how to import lots of things like hyper links form my website,

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:24.000
it’s a little more code, but let’s needlessly crawl out a couple of levels then make a graph of what that corner of the web looks like.

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.000
You know, you can make symbolic pure functions to do all sorts of things.

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:31.000
Like this takes the list of English words that are built into the Wolfram language

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:36.000
and creates a function to find which one is nearest to a given word.

00:06:36.000 --> 00:06:41.000
Now you can pick up this function and apply it to any word and make a simple spell corrector.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:49.000
Pure functions and functional programming get in everywhere, here’s selecting palindrome words based on a simple symbolic pure function.

00:06:49.000 --> 00:06:55.000
You can generalize this dramatically and for example get a shockingly powerful symbolic way

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:58.000
to query databases and large hierarchical collections of data.

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:03.000
In a sense, the Wolfram language is the ultimate fusion language

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:07.000
because its symbolic nature lets it encompass pretty much any style of programming,

00:07:07.000 --> 00:07:12.000
one that’s very powerful and kind of uniquely symbolic is pattern based programming.

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:17.000
The idea is you give a pattern for transforming some class of symbolic expressions.

00:07:17.000 --> 00:07:22.000
Like this says that any time there’s f of some particular nested list structure, here’s the value.

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:30.000
So now if we give f this kind of structure, it’ll produce a result but if the pattern doesn’t match, it will just stay symbolic.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:33.000
And this is basically how all function definitions in the Wolfram language work.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:40.000
The Wolfram language is a huge language, by far the largest ever, but it’s built on a small set of powerful principles.

00:07:40.000 --> 00:07:45.000
The first is coherence, the idea that everything in the language must fit together.

00:07:45.000 --> 00:07:50.000
I must say that I’ve spent a fair fraction of the past 30 years working to make this happen,

00:07:50.000 --> 00:07:54.000
designing every corner of the language so it’s as consistent and unified and integrated as possible.

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:02.000
So it’s easy to understand and so one can take something from over here and be confident that it’ll feed right into something over there.

00:08:02.000 --> 00:08:09.000
I’ve had another principle too, maximum automation, the idea that the language should take care of as much as possible.

00:08:09.000 --> 00:08:15.000
Let’s say that you are doing machine learning and you want to build a data classifier. Well there are all sorts of methods for doing that,

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:24.000
but in the Wolfram language there’s just one super function, Classify, packed with meta algorithms to automatically figure out what to do.

00:08:24.000 --> 00:08:29.000
Of course if you want you can go in and control every detail but otherwise you can just treat it like a black box,

00:08:29.000 --> 00:08:37.000
one of thousands of super functions in the Wolfram language that effectively give you the highest possible level building blocks for programs.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:42.000
And those building blocks don’t just contain algorithms, they’ve got knowledge and data too.

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:45.000
Knowledge about how to import and export hundreds of formats,

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:49.000
knowledge, about how to interact with all sorts of external API’s,

00:08:49.000 --> 00:08:52.000
knowledge about how to compute properties of systems in the world,

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:59.000
and also huge amounts of curated computable data stored in the Wolfram cloud and continuously updated.

00:08:59.000 --> 00:09:03.000
The same data that powers Wolfram Alpha, completely programmatically accessible.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:10.000
But ok, so the Wolfram language makes it really easy to create really powerful programs, but what can you do with them.

00:09:11.000 --> 00:09:14.000
Well it turns out that the symbolic nature of the language does something great here too

00:09:14.000 --> 00:09:18.000
because it lets the language define and describe its own deployment.

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.000
Let’s say you set up a function in the Wolfram language, you can run it in your notebook session,

00:09:23.000 --> 00:09:27.000
or you can just take the symbolic code and deploy it to the cloud.

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:32.000
You can just go to the cloud object from the web, and if you do, you’ll see a web interface

00:09:32.000 --> 00:09:36.000
which automatically runs your Wolfram language code in the cloud.

00:09:36.000 --> 00:09:39.000
And by the way you can set up a mobile app to do the same kind of thing.

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:44.000
The interface here uses CDF, our computable document format running in the cloud.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:50.000
And in fact you can run the whole native notebook interface for the Wolfram language in the cloud, here it is.

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:55.000
You can use notebooks as templates for reports that get filled in by running Wolfram language code,

00:09:55.000 --> 00:10:01.000
you can schedule Wolfram language code to run on cloud servers at particular time, say, sending you mail.

00:10:01.000 --> 00:10:08.000
You can take any Wolfram language function and set up an instant API for it that you can call from anywhere on the web.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:12.000
Like this asks for a JSON output of some weather data for the users geo location.

00:10:13.000 --> 00:10:18.000
If you have an embedded Wolfram engine, you can get code to embed a call to this in lots of languages

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:21.000
and you can actually call the code without even going to the web.

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:28.000
One thing about the Wolfram language is that it’s completely scalable, you can write tiny programs that are already very powerful

00:10:28.000 --> 00:10:32.000
or you can write multimillion line programs like Wolfram Alpha.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:38.000
It’s so much easier to build and debug symbolic programs because at each stage, every piece is meaningful

00:10:39.000 --> 00:10:42.000
and depending on your style of programming, there are various IDE’s

00:10:42.000 --> 00:10:46.000
for the Wolfram language that take advantage of the symbolic character of the language.

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:52.000
You can use the Wolfram language for high performance computing too; it’s got parallelism built right into the language.

00:10:53.000 --> 00:10:58.000
So this is automatically starting up and running lots of different processors across my little heterogeneous network

00:10:58.000 --> 00:11:00.000
and collecting the results.

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:05.000
You can also use the Wolfram language on the upcoming generation of tiny embedded processors,

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:08.000
and for example it’s included with the OS for the raspberry pi Linux computer.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:15.000
You can use the Wolfram language to interact with devices, in fact, the Wolfram language is in a unique position with the respect to devices

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:21.000
and with respect of real world data in general because in a sense it’s a language that has a model of the world built in.

00:11:21.000 --> 00:11:25.000
and in fact, WDF, the Wolfram Data Framework, encapsulates that model,

00:11:25.000 --> 00:11:29.000
and within the Wolfram language, there are lots of ways of taking unstructured data

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:35.000
and getting it into WDF with its units and dates and entities and so on make canonical.

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.000
When it comes to devices, the Wolfram connect to devices project is curating them,

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:43.000
to make them automatically accessible from within the language.

00:11:43.000 --> 00:11:49.000
Ok so I’ve tried to give some indication of what the Wolfram language is about, so how can you get started?

00:11:50.000 --> 00:11:55.000
Like after every output, there’s a suggestion bar that suggests what you might want to do next

00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:00.000
and you can even try just giving input in natural language and staring to construct your programs that way,

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:05.000
and when you’ve got a program you like, you can deploy it in all sorts of ways.

00:12:06.000 --> 00:12:10.000
My goal with the Wolfram language has been to encapsulate as much computation and knowledge as possible

00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:15.000
so people can go from ideas to deployed products as quickly and easily as possible.

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:20.000
I’ve also been very pleased that the Wolfram language seems to be uniquely good as a first programming language

00:12:20.000 --> 00:12:26.000
because you can do so much so easily and quickly get exposed to such a broad range of methods and topics.

00:12:26.000 --> 00:12:29.000
I’ve been working towards the Wolfram language for 30 years.

00:12:29.000 --> 00:12:36.000
We laid strong foundations very early and we’ve just kept on building and actually I keep on being amazed at what’s possible

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:43.000
and now we’ve got a vast stack of technology and content that I think just completely changes how we should think about programming.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:45.000
I get a big kick out of using it every day,

00:12:45.000 --> 00:12:50.000
I hope you’ll have a chance to try it and that you’ll end up doing amazing things with the Wolfram language.

00:12:50.000 --> 00:12:52.000
Thanks very much.

